WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Speaker 1: Brian Stelter is out of a job at CNN&nbsp;
with the network deciding to cancel his weekend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.800
program. Reliable Sources, a show&nbsp;
that's been on the air for 30 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.720
the last nine of which Brian Stelter hosted. And&nbsp;
this is part of CNN's attempts to rebrand itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.680
We know that CNN used to try to play the role&nbsp;
of being a down the line corporate media outlet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.760
positioning itself as the rational center between&nbsp;
Fox News on the right and MSNBC on the left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.560
going with the most trusted name in news,&nbsp;
Monica. Then the Trump years rolled around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.600
and they shifted into being a more outwardly&nbsp;
anti-Trump network, more opinionated, becoming a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.200
lot more just like an MSNBC alternative. But now,&nbsp;
with some changes over at CNN with Jeff Zucker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.960
out with Chris Cuomo, out with CNN. Plus, failing&nbsp;
miserably. They have the room to make some image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.360
changes and it looks like they're going back&nbsp;
in the direction of being a more hard news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.160
down the line network. And I'm sure they'll still&nbsp;
be worthy of plenty of criticism, right, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.040
they probably will continue to try to be neutral&nbsp;
instead of objective. They'll be too corporate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.560
too sensationalist, focus on two stories. But&nbsp;
to me at least, I do think that this makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.200
for them to go in this direction because there&nbsp;
seems to be more market share trying to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:17.760
centrist news outlet with so many other cable&nbsp;
news outlets being left or right. And it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.840
like the change is largely being influenced&nbsp;
by John Malone, who is the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.840
investor over at Warner Brothers Discovery Inc.&nbsp;
That's the parent company of CNN. And Malone is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.960
right slash libertarian guy. He's not a Trumpist,&nbsp;
but he's definitely a conservative. He actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.440
likes to watch Fox News, reportedly. And I don't&nbsp;
know if you've seen this, but over at Fox, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.520
absolutely love to bash Brian Stelter whenever&nbsp;
given the opportunity. So it makes sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.640
after seeing all that Fox News that Malone&nbsp;
would want to go in the direction of ousting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.120
Brian Stelter in favor of someone else. And,&nbsp;
you know, just with the network trying to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.280
all these changes, generally there was just no&nbsp;
room for Stelter, unfortunately for him. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.920
there's also the money angle, too, right? Because&nbsp;
Stelter was reportedly making almost $1,000,000 a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.320
year, despite the fact that his show was only&nbsp;
getting 500000 to 600000 viewers per episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.600
So looking at the business side of things, it just&nbsp;
doesn't seem to add up. And final point I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.280
to make on this. I do like the idea of having a&nbsp;
media literacy show out there, something with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.920
idea of being focused on the media, a watchdog&nbsp;
for the media. But is CNN the right place to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.760
have a show like this? Because you can't pretend&nbsp;
to be the arbiter of good journalistic practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.680
when it comes to other networks, when you yourself&nbsp;
at your network are also going to be victims of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.840
the same sort of biases and problems. Right.&nbsp;
You're just not in a position where you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.200
the credibility to do a show like that. And it&nbsp;
often would create this conflict of interest where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.600
you don't cover your same your own network with&nbsp;
the same sort of scrutiny that you'll cover other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.160
networks. Like I remember when CNN Plus failed,&nbsp;
Stelter went on the airwaves that he talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:00.160
how it was really too early to tell whether or not&nbsp;
CNN Plus was a success because it only made it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.280
couple of weeks or a couple of days, whatever it&nbsp;
was. And so it was just too early to tell. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:11.440
if MSNBC or Fox News's streaming network failed&nbsp;
so early, would he have had the same commentary? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.840
don't think so. Something tells me his takes would&nbsp;
have been a little bit different. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.760
you can just decide not to cover what's&nbsp;
going on at your network at all. So it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.520
created all these conflicts. I just don't&nbsp;
think that CNN was the right place for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.000
And that is the latest reliable source is&nbsp;
canceled after 30 years, the last nine of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.360
which Brian Stelter hosted. And now he's out&nbsp;
of a job looking for somewhere else to work

